# Plan 11a / D14 — the production edge.
#
# Three jobs: terminate TLS on the real hostname, serve the built SPA, and reverse-proxy the API
# path prefixes (including the WebSocket upgrade) to the api container.
#
# THE UPGRADE NEEDS NOTHING SPECIAL. Spike question E measured it rather than assuming it: a bare
# `reverse_proxy` produced HTTP 101 with a `sec-websocket-accept` header, the gateway then ran its
# own auth protocol over the proxied socket (auth_timeout frame, close 4001), a direct-connection
# control behaved byte-identically, and a wrong-path upgrade came back 502. No `header_up`, no
# transport block, no websocket matcher.
#
# TLS: automatic. Caddy provisions and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for the site address
# below over ACME. The HTTP→HTTPS redirect on :80 is now the explicit `http://` site at the foot of
# this file, and the reason is one header: Caddy's own automatic redirect stamps `Server: Caddy` on
# every 308 and nothing in the Caddyfile can reach it (WASA L-09). This file used to say an explicit
# `http://` block would "take :80 away from Caddy's own automatic redirect and ACME handling for no
# gain" — the gain is now named, and the ACME half does not happen: Caddy answers HTTP-01
# challenges in the server itself, BEFORE any user route runs, so a site on :80 cannot shadow them
# (and TLS-ALPN-01 on :443 is untouched either way). The block redirects every host, as the
# automatic one did, with the same 308 and the same Location. Proved in throwaway caddy:2-alpine
# containers, never against production: the 308 and its Location unchanged for the named host and
# for an unknown one, and an HTTP-01 order validated end to end against a private ACME CA with
# TLS-ALPN-01 disabled, so HTTP-01 through this file was the only way it could succeed.
#
# There is NO auth gate in front of the app. Plan 02's auth is the gate; an IP allowlist is a
# Decisions-§ option the owner can turn on later in one directive.
#
# D4/GC1 portability: on on-prem metal this file is RE-POINTED (a different hostname, or an
# internal CA) and never rewritten.
{
	# HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 only. Left at the default, Caddy also opens HTTP/3 on 443/UDP and
	# advertises it with `alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=2592000` — measured on the first bring-up. The
	# compose file publishes 443/TCP only, matching a host firewall that admits 80/tcp and
	# 443/tcp, so that advertisement is a promise nothing can keep: clients cache it for 30 days
	# and race a QUIC connection that never arrives before falling back. Publishing the UDP port
	# and opening it is the other coherent answer; delete this block if you take it.
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
	}
	# WASA L-09: the :80 redirect is the explicit `http://` site at the foot of this file, because
	# Caddy's automatic one sends `Server: Caddy` and cannot be told not to. This turns off ONLY the
	# redirect — certificate management and the ACME challenges are untouched.
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCESS LOGGING — ADDED BY THE 2026-09 POST-DEPLOY SWEEP, BECAUSE EDGE 5xx WAS INVISIBLE.
#
# There was no `log` directive here at all, and that does NOT mean "logs to stderr": with none,
# Caddy's access logging is OFF and `docker logs caddy` carries lifecycle events only. So "no
# 5xx in the caddy log" proved nothing during the sweep — the log had never held a request line
# in its life — and live probes had to be substituted for the evidence this should have been.
#
# THIS WIDENS WHAT THE EDGE WRITES DOWN, deliberately: every request the site handles now lands
# on disk, `uri` included, so `/api/patients/<id>` paths and client IPs are in a file. The PATH is
# NOT redacted and must not be, since it is most of what makes a 5xx line diagnosable. Read the
# file as what it is: PHI-adjacent.
#
# ═══ WASA M-04 — WHAT THE FILTER REMOVES, AND WHY THE LIST IS NOT CADDY'S ═══
#
# Caddy's own redaction covers `Authorization` and `Cookie` and NOTHING ELSE. Measured on
# caddy:2-alpine v2.11.4 in a throwaway container: a request carrying `X-Agent-Key` and
# `X-Totp-Code` was written here with both values in the clear — and the print relay sends its
# agent key on every poll. So the filter below replaces, with REDACTED, every request header the
# API reads (`guards.ts` and every `@Headers(...)`) plus `Cookie`. The two Caddy already redacted
# are listed too, so a future `log_credentials` cannot quietly reopen them. REPLACE rather than
# delete: "was a key sent at all?" is a real diagnostic question, and the header NAME answers it.
#
# The list is PINNED BY `apps/core/test/caddyfile-hardening.test.ts`, which derives the headers from
# the API's source — a new credential header in the app with no line here turns it red.
#
# The QUERY VALUES of `q`, `text`, `complaint` and `term` are replaced too: they are the free text
# a clerk types — a patient's name into `/api/patients/search?q=`, a complaint into
# `/api/opd/cds/recognise/complaint?text=` — and none of them helps diagnose a 5xx. The keys stay.
#
# `pt` is replaced for a harder reason: it is a CREDENTIAL. ABDM's HIU data push is authenticated by
# a 256-bit token in the address we give ABDM, `/api/abdm/callbacks/hiu/data-push?pt=<token>`. It is
# a query parameter, not a path segment, precisely so this filter can remove it — Caddy allows one
# filter per field, and the path is kept on purpose. Measured on caddy:2-alpine v2.11.4: the line
# records `…/data-push?pt=REDACTED`. The test above also refuses any ABDM route with a `:token` path.
#
# ONE SNIPPET, TWO SITES: the HTTPS site and the :80 redirect both `import access_log`, so the 308s
# land in the same file through the same filter, as they did when the automatic redirect server
# inherited this site's logger.
#
# WHY /data RATHER THAN /var/log/caddy: this container's only writable mounts are
# `caddy_data:/data` and `caddy_config:/config` — `./caddy` is `:ro` and is config. Anywhere
# else is the container's ephemeral filesystem, so a deploy that recreated caddy would erase the
# log at exactly the moment somebody wants to read it; that is the reasoning `pgbackrest_log`
# was created under, applied here. Caddy runs as root in this image and creates the missing
# directory itself (0700) — confirmed in a throwaway caddy:2-alpine, never against production.
#
# THE ROLL NUMBERS ARE LOAD-BEARING, NOT HYGIENE: `caddy_data` also holds the ACME account key
# and the issued certificate, so a log left to grow would take TLS down with it. 10 MiB active +
# 5 rolled bounds it at ~60 MiB even if nothing compressed; rolled files are gzipped, measured
# at ~30:1 on these lines (1 MiB of them → 33 KB), so the real footprint is ~12 MiB. Lines run
# ~335 bytes, so a roll is ~31k requests — days of this hospital, not hours. 720h is retention,
# which outlives a deploy-to-sweep cycle by a wide margin without turning the volume into an
# archive; whichever of size, count and age bites first wins.
#
# `roll_keep_for`, NOT `roll_keep_days`: the JSON field is `roll_keep_days`, but the Caddyfile
# adapter knows only the duration form and rejects the other outright — and a config this file
# cannot adapt is deploy.sh step 6's `caddy reload` failing AFTER the build and the migrations.
# `720h` adapts to `"roll_keep_days": 30` (checked with `caddy adapt`).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(access_log) {
	log {
		output file /data/logs/access.log {
			roll_size 10MiB
			roll_keep 5
			roll_keep_for 720h
		}
		# JSON so the sweep can ask a machine rather than eyeball it: every line carries `status`,
		# and a 5xx also carries `"level":"error"`, which is the grep that "no 5xx in the caddy log"
		# was supposed to have been all along. `wrap json` keeps exactly that encoding.
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>headers>Authorization replace REDACTED
				request>headers>Cookie replace REDACTED
				request>headers>X-Agent-Key replace REDACTED
				request>headers>X-Totp-Code replace REDACTED
				request>headers>Idempotency-Key replace REDACTED
				request>uri query {
					replace q REDACTED
					replace text REDACTED
					replace complaint REDACTED
					replace term REDACTED
					replace pt REDACTED
				}
			}
		}
	}
}

hmis.crkmch.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	import access_log

	# Security headers, applied to every response the site produces — proxied and static alike.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()"
		# WASA L-09: neither the edge nor the proxy hop names its software. `-Via` because
		# `reverse_proxy` stamps `via: 1.1 Caddy` on every /api response (measured).
		-Server
		-Via
	}

	# WASA L-09 — CADDY'S OWN ERRORS. The `header` block above never reaches a response Caddy
	# raises itself (a 405 for `OPTIONS /`, a 502 with the api down): those skip the handler chain
	# and went out with `Server: Caddy`. The status is Caddy's own and the body stays empty, exactly
	# what was served before this block existed.
	handle_errors {
		header -Server
		respond "" {err.status_code}
	}

	# Access logging: see the `(access_log)` snippet above the site — WASA M-04's filter lives there.

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# THE PARITY LINE — PLAN 11g / DD1. ONE PREFIX, AND THE ORIGIN NOW HAS ONE RULE:
	# `/api/*` IS THE API, EVERYTHING ELSE IS THE APPLICATION.
	#
	# This line used to list twelve prefixes mirrored from the SPA's dev proxy — /auth /patients
	# /approvals /workflow /health /opd /billing /alerts /admin /ops /tariff /ws — and every one
	# of them was ALSO a path apps/web/src/router.tsx declares a SCREEN on. This matcher is
	# path-only (no method, no Accept), and it is evaluated FIRST, so a browser asking for the
	# /admin/users SCREEN was handed the API's {"statusCode":401} and 15 of the 20 screens did not
	# load at all. That is the 2026-08-24 synthetic smoke test's D1, and it could not reproduce in
	# dev because the vite server resolved the same collision the other way.
	#
	# Still pinned by apps/core/test/caddyfile-parity.test.ts, which now also asserts that NO SPA
	# route falls inside the proxied space — the leg that would have caught it.
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	@api path /api*

	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated in the order written, which is what
	# this site needs: without them Caddy's directive ordering would run `try_files` (a rewrite)
	# BEFORE `reverse_proxy`, rewrite /api/health to /index.html because no such file exists under
	# the static root, and quietly stop the api from ever being reached.
	handle @api {
		# The prefix is a fact about the ORIGIN, not about the API: it is stripped here so the
		# api container keeps its own path space (`@Controller("billing")`, the compose
		# healthcheck on /health, the Prometheus scrape, and ~30 supertest e2e suites all still
		# speak the unprefixed path). `uri` runs in the rewrite phase, before `reverse_proxy`.
		uri strip_prefix /api
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# The SPA. /srv is where the Dockerfile's `web` target puts the vite build (T1-1); the
	# index.html fallback is what makes client-side routing survive a hard refresh.
	handle {
		root * /srv
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}

# WASA L-09 — THE :80 REDIRECT, WRITTEN OUT. See the file header: Caddy's automatic redirect
# stamps `Server: Caddy` on its 308 and no directive reaches it, so `auto_https disable_redirects`
# (global options) switches it off and this block is the redirect. MEASURED, the other way first:
# with the automatic one left on, Caddy inserted its own host-matched redirect AHEAD of this
# catch-all and the banner stayed on exactly the request that matters. `http://` with no host
# answers every Host, as the automatic catch-all did, and `{host}{uri}` is the same Location.
# ACME HTTP-01 challenges are still answered by Caddy itself before this route runs.
http:// {
	import access_log
	header -Server
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}
